<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Luxembourg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> was a dynasty of counts of Luxembourg that rose to the first rank of European royalty in the fourteenth century. It produced the emperors Henry VII, Charles IV and Sigismund, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> from 1310 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1387, and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> from 1373. The male line ended with Sigismund in 1437, and most of its lands passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-luxembourg.jpg" alt="House of Luxembourg" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wereszczyński · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COA_Luxembourg_with_crest.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The royal House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> were not male-line descendants of the original counts of Luxembourg, a branch of the House of Ardenne that had ruled the county since the late tenth century. They descended from the House of Limburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arlon">Arlon</a>, whose members had been dukes of Lower Lorraine in the eleventh century. In 1247 Henry, a younger son of Duke Waleran III of Limburg, inherited the county on the death of his mother Countess Ermesinde and became Count Henry V of Luxembourg.</p><p>His grandson Henry VII became count when his father, Henry VI, died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worringen">Battle of Worringen</a> in 1288. In 1308, after the murder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> king Albert I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, Henry was elected Rex Romanorum. His brother Archbishop-Elector Baldwin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> backed and orchestrated the election, and Henry prevailed against Charles, Count of Valois.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1310 Henry VII arranged the marriage of his son John to the Přemyslid heiress Elisabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, through whom the family acquired the Kingdom of Bohemia and could compete better with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> and Wittelsbach dynasties. Henry was crowned emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and died in 1313 while campaigning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>, disturbed by the family's rise, passed over John, and Louis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a> took the Empire. John concentrated on Bohemia and gradually made the Piast dukes of Silesia his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> from 1327 to 1335.</p><p>His son Charles IV came to the imperial throne in 1346, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> served for centuries as a constitution of the Empire. Charles acquired the duchies of Brabant and Limburg, the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>, and in 1373 the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. Decline began under his son Wenceslaus, whom the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> deposed in 1400 in favour of the Wittelsbach Rupert. In 1410 Wenceslaus' brother Sigismund took over, stabilised the family's rule and helped to end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Western Schism</a> in 1417. Sigismund was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> from 1387 through his wife Mary and was crowned emperor in 1433.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Sigismund left no male heirs, and with his death in 1437 the senior branch of the dynasty became extinct. His son-in-law, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> archduke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-of-germany">Albert V of Austria</a>, succeeded him. Sigismund's only surviving child Elizabeth had married Albert in 1422, and she conveyed the major part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> inheritance to the Habsburgs and, through her daughter Elisabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, later to the Jagiellons. The line of the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> continued for a time: George of Luxembourg, who died in 1457, is the last known member of the family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Luxembourg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Ascanian line died out in 1320 the Mark passed to the Wittelsbachs in 1323 and to the <b>Luxembourgs</b> in 1373.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
